<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sotirios Panagiotis Chytas
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>chytas@wisc.edu, <a href="https://github.com/SPChytas/ReCo" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/SPChytas/ReCo</a></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Wisconsin-Madison
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Miso Choi
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Korea University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hyunwoo J. Kim
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>KAIST
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vikas Singh
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Wisconsin-Madison
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision Language Models (VLMs) show impressive
capabilities in integrating and reasoning with both
visual and language data.
But these models make mistakes.
A common finding – similar to LLMs – is their tendency to hallucinate, i.e., generate plausible sounding
text which is not grounded in the visual input, or at worst,
is contradictory.
A growing consensus attributes this behavior to
an over-reliance on language – especially as the generation progresses, the model suffers from
a “fading memory effect” with respect to the provided visual input.
We study mechanisms by which this behavior can
be controlled. Specifically,
using ideas from geometric algebra and relational compositions, we propose the addition of a small, trainable module
(named ReCo) on top of any VLM – no other modification is needed.
We show that such a lightweight module is able to mitigate the fading memory effect on three of the most widely used VLMs (InstructBLIP, LlaVA, MiniGPT4), where we see
performance improvements on multiple benchmarks.
Additionally, we show that our module can be combined with many of the other approaches for reducing hallucination where we achieve improved results for each one.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><img src="2506.22636v1/figures/figures.001.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/171;" width="215" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> before and after ReCo. We propose a small module that, with minimal training, it is able to effectively reduce the hallucination rate of widely used VLMs.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the advances in the capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs), recent efforts have sought
to extend these models to the multi-modality setting, i.e., processing and “understanding”
additional modalities beyond text such as audio, images, and videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
To this end, one milestone is the development of Vision Language Models (VLMs)
that can accept both images and natural language as input, and generate contextually meaningful outputs for tasks, including visual question answering and image captioning.
Some prominent models are InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, MiniGPT4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and Llava <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, that show strong <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image+text</span> understanding skills.
However, we know that VLMs piggyback
heavily on the core capabilities of the parent LLM to which the visual representations have been aligned. This endows sizable compute benefits –
InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> costs about <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> GPU hours, while Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> (the parent LLM), needed <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="180000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>180000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">180000</annotation></semantics></math> GPU hours.
But this VLM/LLM dependence means that VLMs also inherit known weaknesses of LLMs and sometimes, these weaknesses can be magnified. One example is hallucination, i.e., generating text that is plausible but does not accurately reflect the provided input.
More than a handful of results in the literature show that in many cases, a VLM
appears to ignore the image and generates a description that is not influenced much at all by this extra visual input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, although this is an extreme case of hallucination.
We show an example in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> “sees” multiple cars and trucks, although we only see an ambulance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:42%;"><img src="2506.22636v1/figures/figures.009.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:205/95;" width="205" height="95" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S1.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The “fading memory effect”. <span id="S1.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top</span>: First-layer attention of the visual and textual input as the generation progresses.
<span id="S1.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom</span>: The effect of the visual input on the logits distribution as the generation progresses. We calculate the next token prediction with and without the visual input and we compute the distributional difference. After the first tokens, the next token can be predicted just from the previously generated text.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Available mitigation mechanisms?</span> Mitigation mechanisms for the foregoing
problem
can be classified into two categories:</p>
<span id="S1.I1" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item"><span id="S1.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span></span> <span id="S1.I1.i1.3" class="ltx_text">train-based methods, where the VLM is further finetuned with different loss functions and/or using more suitable datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>; and
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item"><span id="S1.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span></span> <span id="S1.I1.i2.3" class="ltx_text">rule-based methods, where the VLM remains frozen but a new/improved
generation process must be adopted. Some
proposals treat the model as a black box without any access to its attention maps and its parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, whereas others use them to steer the model’s generation process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.
</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Why do VLMs hallucinate?</span> The literature suggests that VLMs over-rely on language priors, gradually “forgetting" the visual input as text generation progresses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
If next token
generation is implicitly
probing an internal conditional probability distribution,
<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\circlerighthalfblack" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="undefined">\circlerighthalfblack</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\circlerighthalfblack</annotation></semantics></math>), where the conditioning is with respect to both
the text <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the image <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> with the right balance <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circlerighthalfblack" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="undefined">\circlerighthalfblack</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circlerighthalfblack</annotation></semantics></math>, progressively
the image’s role diminishes
to <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\circleurquadblack)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="undefined">\circleurquadblack</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\circleurquadblack)</annotation></semantics></math>
and finally to <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\mdwhtcircle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mtext class="undefined">\mdwhtcircle</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(\cdot|\mdwhtcircle)</annotation></semantics></math>,
an effect that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> calls the <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“fading memory effect”</span>.
We further probe this behavior: in <a href="#S1.F2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (top), we can observe that during the generation process, the total attention on the visual tokens drops significantly, showing that the model tends to “ignore” this information and rely on the language prior as it generates more and more text. In <a href="#S1.F2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (bottom), following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we compute the difference in the logits distribution with/without the image. If <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the text generated so far and <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the visual input, we calculate the difference between the probability distributions <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(y_{t+1}|T_{t},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(y_{t+1}|T_{t},I)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(y_{t+1}|T_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(y_{t+1}|T_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the next token prediction.
Using the Hellinger distance, we see that the distance drops to almost <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> after the first <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> tokens: the image has practically no effect on the generated tokens afterwards.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:44%;"><img src="2506.22636v1/figures/figures.011.png" id="S1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/182;" width="215" height="182" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S1.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">VLM: ideal versus practice. On the left, we show what actually happens, where at each timestep the image’s influence (<math id="S1.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>) in orange is diminished compared to the text <math id="S1.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> in blue. So, the generated text is not an accurate representation of the visual input. An ideal VLM (right side) would form an object (<math id="S1.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>) that
perfectly encapsulates <span id="S1.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> of the given input, leading to accurate generation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The desired behavior.</span> A potential mitigation strategy will involve a “correct” composition of both the visual and the textual embeddings. But we should avoid overhauling the
entire VLM architecture if possible.
If a VLM estimates the probability distribution <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}(y_{t+1}|B_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}(y_{t+1}|B_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (shown as <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circlerighthalfblack" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="undefined">\circlerighthalfblack</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circlerighthalfblack</annotation></semantics></math> above) encapsulates all the required information for the next token, we would expect that the quantity <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> combines (or <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">composes</span>) both <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, and is sensitive to changes in <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> of the two inputs for all <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this does not always happen in practice – so we must intervene and carefully design <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> so that neither of the inputs “get lost” (<a href="#S1.F3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compositionality and Geometric Algebra.</span>
Both</p>
<span id="S1.I2" class="ltx_inline-enumerate">
<span id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item"><span id="S1.I2.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span></span> <span id="S1.I2.i1.3" class="ltx_text">compositional learning and
</span></span>
<span id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_inline-item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_inline-item"><span id="S1.I2.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span></span> <span id="S1.I2.i2.3" class="ltx_text">geometric algebra
</span></span>
</span>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">are
mature topics of research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and inform our approach.
<span id="S1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compositionality</span> means that complex expressions are based on the meaning of their constituents and rules for combination. This was central to various visual understanding tasks: recognizing combinations of attributes and objects (e.g., “spotted giraffe” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>), understanding object interactions (e.g., “person holding umbrella” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>), identifying transformations (e.g., “broken glass” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>), and parsing complex scenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
Composition is not widely used as a standalone solution, but it is
relevant in interpretability and checking
semantic validity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geometric algebra</span> generalizes concepts like complex numbers, quaternions, and vector algebra into a single framework for writing down geometric relationships/transformations. While early applications were in physics, the ability to manipulate geometric objects through algebraic operations like the geometric product (captures inner <span id="S1.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span> outer product) is finding use in graphics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and, more recently, in machine learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span> This paper provides mitigation strategies for the fading memory effect by leveraging the compositional concepts above. We propose <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReCo</span>, a lightweight module which is data-driven but treats the VLM as a black box, combining the best of both worlds. ReCo can be easily deployed on top of any VLM during/after training and, with minimal effort, improves their hallucination behavior. We achieve promising improvements on three widely used VLMs (InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, LlaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and MiniGPT4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>) across multiple benchmarks and datasets (POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, CHAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, AMBER <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, HallusionBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, and MME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>).
Despite the small size/compute footprint,
we get a performance boost without any increase in the inference time of the VLMs.
Furthermore, ReCo can be combined with virtually any of the rule-based methods without any modifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries: Geometric Algebra</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Geometric (or Clifford) Algebra (GA), an extension of linear algebra, deals with a ring <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a set of elements <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> accompanied by two operations: <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. The elements of GA (i.e., the elements of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>) are also vectors – <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> contains scalars (or <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors), “typical” vectors (or <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors) as well as <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors, <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors, and so on. The subset of <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> that contains all <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors is usually denoted as <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-vectors.</span> A <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vector can be defined as the geometric product of <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> orthogonal <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors, i.e., if <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{v}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{v}_{k}\}\in\mathcal{G}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{v}_{1},\cdots,\mathbf{v}_{k}\}\in\mathcal{G}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}\perp\mathbf{v}_{j},\ \forall i\neq j\in[k]\times[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}\perp\mathbf{v}_{j},\ \forall i\neq j\in[k]\times[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{v}_{k}=\mathbf{v}^{(k)}\in\mathcal{G}_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{v}_{k}=\mathbf{v}^{(k)}\in\mathcal{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, each <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-vector can be defined as the “wedge” (<math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\wedge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∧</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\wedge</annotation></semantics></math>) product of <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-vectors, where the wedge product is:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge\mathbf{v}_{k}=\frac{1}{k!}\bigoplus_{\sigma}\text{sign}(\sigma)\otimes\mathbf{v}_{\sigma(1)}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{v}_{\sigma(k)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>σ</mi></munder><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge\mathbf{v}_{k}=\frac{1}{k!}\bigoplus_{\sigma}\text{sign}(\sigma)\otimes\mathbf{v}_{\sigma(1)}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{v}_{\sigma(k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a permutation from the symmetric group. The sign is <math id="S2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>) for even (or odd) permutations and the following equivalence holds:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{v}_{k}=\mathbf{v}_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge\mathbf{v}_{k}\Leftrightarrow\mathbf{v}_{1}\perp\cdots\perp\mathbf{v}_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>∧</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">⟂</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⟂</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{1}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{v}_{k}=\mathbf{v}_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge\mathbf{v}_{k}\Leftrightarrow\mathbf{v}_{1}\perp\cdots\perp\mathbf{v}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geometric operations.</span> A 2-vector represents a plane, a 3-vector a 3D object, and so on—unlike the inner product, which reduces vectors to a scalar with no recovery of inputs. The geometric product resembles and generalizes the outer product. These GA operations and multi-vectors motivate our instantiation: a systematic method to <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fuse</span> or <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">compose</span> vectors beyond the common weighted averaging used in most architectures – this is the relevance to our “reminder composition”.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Practical implementations and limitations.</span>
While GA gives us the necessary axioms/properties,
it does not provide a specific, practical instantiation. In general, a correct (but efficient) implementation in low dimensions (<math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 6</annotation></semantics></math>) requires intensive
effort, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.
In general, since we deal with vectors, the typical choice would be the tensor product, which is infeasible for high dimensional vectors or multi-products (exponential memory), see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>, where quaternions
are used for a computer graphics use case.
We shift our focus to ideas that can scale while preserving, to the extent possible, the general framework of GA.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vector Symbolic Architectures.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> recently introduced ways to combine vectors beyond linear combinations. Earlier works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> formalized this under <span id="S2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vector Symbolic Architectures</span> (VSA), named for their use of high-dimensional vectors and operations (<math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes,\ \oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo rspace="0.278em">,</mo><mo>⊕</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes,\ \oplus</annotation></semantics></math>) resembling bind and bundle in symbolic and connectionist AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. For a detailed overview, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. Recent works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> have linked these ideas to physics, particularly Fock Space, connecting them to Geometric Algebra. This allows us to adopt these operations directly for our application.

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relevance of these concepts.</span>
Our main hypothesis is that the <span id="S2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fading memory effect</span> can be mitigated through a <span id="S2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">composition</span> of the textual and visual information. The logical question then is how this composition can be designed, say, first conceptually and then for practical use. Theoretically, GA provides us a structured framework for composition problems, enabling the integration of modalities such as image and text, <span id="S2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">without</span> the need of ad-hoc solutions. Practically, VSA offers efficient implementations with favorable computational complexity, memory usage, and noise tolerance while preserving GA’s properties.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Reminder Composition (ReCo)</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview.</span> We outline our construction to minimize the <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fading memory effect</span>, by calculating <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as an explicit composition of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S1.F3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
If the embedding <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be designed
to be an (almost) lossless multi-vector composition of the generated text and the input image, we will achieve improvements.
Our simple idea is to explicitly form a proper multi-vector of the text and the image information <span id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> decoding the embedding to the token space.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><img src="2506.22636v1/figures/figures.008.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:238/221;" width="238" height="221" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">ReCo overview<span id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. The VLM is treated as a black box, modifying the next token embedding by combining the multi-vector of visual tokens and the current token prediction. First, we bundle the image tokens <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{j}]_{j=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{j}]_{j=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> into a single vector <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> and, then, bind it with <math id="S3.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to form <math id="S3.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring the image’s influence. Finally, the prediction head <math id="S3.F4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (also frozen) predicts the next token <math id="S3.F4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T_{t}]_{t=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T_{t}]_{t=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the VLM’s predicted embeddings for each one of the <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> generated tokens which are then fed into the LLM head (<math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>) to form the next token <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[w_{t}]_{t=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[w_{t}]_{t=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the hidden state of the LLM at the step <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> (usually denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> but we use <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> here to mnemonically suggest “text”). Additionally, let <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[I_{j}]_{j=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[I_{j}]_{j=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> output embeddings that correspond to the <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> image embeddings that are fed to the VLM. An ideal VLM should closely capture the composition:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small T_{t}=g\Bigg(T_{t-1}\otimes\bigg(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{M}I_{j}\bigg)\otimes\mathbf{p}_{t}\Bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐩</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small T_{t}=g\Bigg(T_{t-1}\otimes\bigg(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{M}I_{j}\bigg)\otimes\mathbf{p}_{t}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which states that the next token depends on the previous one <span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">composed</span> with all of the provided image information, with <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> denoting a learnable transformation of the multi-vector and <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting some extra optional information about the index of the current token. The next token corresponds to <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}=f(T_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}=f(T_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> but now the image effect, by design, <span id="S3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">cannot</span> be neglected. Notice that (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3 Reminder Composition (ReCo) ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) simply re-formulates what we have already observed about <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{t+1}|T_{t},I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{t+1}|T_{t},I)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{t+1}|T_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{t+1}|T_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S1.F3" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, showing how <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> should actually behave in theory, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\small B_{t}&amp;=g\Bigg(T_{t-1}\otimes\bigg(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{M}I_{j}\bigg)\otimes\mathbf{p}_{t}\Bigg)\\
w_{t}&amp;=f(B_{t})\ \ \propto\mathcal{P}\big(y_{t+1}|B_{t}\big)\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∝</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\small B_{t}&amp;=g\Bigg(T_{t-1}\otimes\bigg(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{M}I_{j}\bigg)\otimes\mathbf{p}_{t}\Bigg)\\
w_{t}&amp;=f(B_{t})\ \ \propto\mathcal{P}\big(y_{t+1}|B_{t}\big)\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we propose to explicitly modify the VLM’s output so that it corresponds to the composition as described in (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3 Reminder Composition (ReCo) ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Based on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, we define the geometric product as the Matrix Binder operation, allowing us to mitigate
the fading memory problem by adding only a small trainable layer on top of a frozen, black-box VLM as:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\small B_{t}=W_{T}T_{t}+W_{I}\Bigg(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{M}I_{j}\Bigg)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">W</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">⨁</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi></munderover><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\small B_{t}=W_{T}T_{t}+W_{I}\Bigg(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{M}I_{j}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The modifications in any VLM’s codebase consist of the addition of <span id="S3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">only two extra lines of code</span> without any other changes to the model’s “internals” (see <a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3 Reminder Composition (ReCo) ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Many composition rules have been proposed in the literature but our choice above was driven by two reasons:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The matrices <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{T},W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{T},W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can be trained alongside the VLM, allowing our modification to be integrated into any model without altering the training process while, at the same time, leveraging the explicit composition rule during inference and generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our modified VLM extends the original model. Setting <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{T}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{T}=I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{I}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{I}=0</annotation></semantics></math> restores the original VLM, making the modified solution space a <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strict superset</span>. Thus, using ReCo in training retains all original capabilities while potentially improving hallucination mitigation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">We should note that our formulation depends on a specific composition strategy. We used one which is mathematically sound but also efficient, but the operations can be upgraded.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:39%;"><img src="2506.22636v1/figures/figures.006.png" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/78;" width="191" height="78" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">ReCo in practice. No access to the LLM is required and the only change is the modification of the prediction head with the addition of a “preprocessing” step.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VLM is a black-box.</span> Notice that (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 Reminder Composition (ReCo) ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and the corresponding code (<a href="#S3.F5" title="In 3 Reminder Composition (ReCo) ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) involves only the output layer of the VLM. This means that, in practice, no access to the model is needed. The output embeddings of the model can be calculated offline, and then we train only the few extra parameters of ReCo in a matter of minutes on any commodity GPU, without even needing to load the entire VLM in memory and performing multiple inference passes through it. This allows us to benefit from additional training data and more suitable training tactics (similar to fine-tuning approaches, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>) while treating the VLM as a frozen black box (similar to decoding strategies, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>). A full comparison with existing solutions is in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before diving into the technical details and comprehensive analysis,, we highlight some of our main findings which we will analyze in detail shortly. These results offer a high-level overview of the most significant outcomes of our work, which will be examined and discussed in depth below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReCo leads to noticeable improvements across <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all</span> of the VLMs we evaluated. In every case, it effectively reduces the rate of hallucinations, demonstrating its robustness and general applicability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReCo works seamlessly with other methods for reducing hallucinations, making it easy to integrate into different systems. Further, when combined with other approaches, it leads to even better performance, showing that the benefits of ReCo and other techniques can complement each other.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReCo enables the model to effectively “recover” from an initial hallucination, distinguishing it from baseline VLMs which often persist in elaborating on hallucinated content. This ability to self-correct contributes to more coherent and accurate model outputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(d)</span></span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our quantitative and qualitative analyses show that ReCo goes beyond just fixing object-related hallucinations. It also helps correct mistakes related to the overall structure of the image and the specific attributes or features of the objects themselves.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22636v1/figures/figures.007.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/117;" width="476" height="117" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">POPE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> results on MiniGPT4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The unmodified version is often unable to comprehend the question and outputs an unrelated answer that does no contain either “Yes” or “No”. On the contrary, ReCo provides the model the ability to answer such questions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.30" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.31" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">CHAIR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> results for both InstructBLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and MiniGPT4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. <span id="S4.T1.31.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Original</span> stands for the unmodified VLM, while VCD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, M3ID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and AvisC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> are the three baselines we consider. In all four models, the addition of ReCo improves significantly the performance as the generation progresses, reducing CHAIR<math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>s</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> as much as <math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\%</annotation></semantics></math> and CHAIR<math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32,\ 64,\ 128,\ 256,\ 512,\ \text{and }1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 64</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 128</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 256</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 512</mn><mo rspace="0.627em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32,\ 64,\ 128,\ 256,\ 512,\ \text{and }1024</annotation></semantics></math> standing for the maximum allowed number of generated tokens.</span></figcaption>
