<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>ReCo vs Others</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ReCo is uniquely placed in the intersection of methods that treat the underlying VLM as a black box (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>) and methods that are training-driven (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>). In <a href="#A1.T4" title="In Appendix A ReCo vs Others ‣ ReCo: Reminder Composition Mitigates Hallucinations in Vision-Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we show the qualitative advantage of ReCo over multiple other proposed solutions for hallucination mitigation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="A1.T4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">ReCo compared to other models.</span></figcaption>
